      <h1 class="blog-title">Radiator Cold at the Top or Bottom? What It Means and How to Fix It</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Where a radiator is cold tells you what is wrong with it. Some fixes take you five minutes with a radiator key. Others need a plumber. Here is how to tell which is which.</p>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span><strong>By</strong> Liverpool Emergency Plumber</span>
        <span><strong>Gas Safe Registered Plumber</strong></span>
        <span>Published 27 November 2026</span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="background:var(--white);padding:2rem 0 3rem;">
    <div class="blog-post">
      <div class="blog-body">
        <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
        <p>Cold at the top and warm at the bottom means air is trapped inside. Bleed the radiator and it should heat up again.</p>
        <p>Cold at the bottom and warm at the top usually means sludge. That is a build-up of rust and dirt from inside the pipes. Bleeding will not fix it. The system needs cleaning.</p>
        <p>Cold all over, while other radiators are hot, usually points to a stuck valve.</p>

        <div class="blog-stat">
          <div class="stat-num">5 min</div>
          <div class="stat-label">About how long it takes to bleed a radiator. All you need is a radiator key and a cloth.</div>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Radiators still cold?" text="Tell us what they are doing and we will tell you the likely fix and price. No call-out fee." />

        <h2>Cold at the Top: Air in the Radiator</h2>
        <p>Air rises, so it collects at the top of the radiator. Hot water cannot reach that part, so it stays cold. You may also hear gurgling in the pipes.</p>
        <p>Air gets in over time, and after any work on the heating. It is the most common radiator fault, and the easiest to fix yourself.</p>

        <h3>How to bleed a radiator</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Turn the heating off</strong> and let the radiators cool. Hot water can spit out of the valve.</li>
          <li><strong>Find the bleed valve.</strong> It is a small square peg in a round fitting, at one of the top corners.</li>
          <li><strong>Hold a cloth under it</strong> and fit a radiator key. You can buy one from any DIY shop.</li>
          <li><strong>Turn the key a quarter turn anticlockwise.</strong> You will hear air hiss out.</li>
          <li><strong>Close it as soon as water comes out.</strong> A steady drip of water means the air has gone.</li>
          <li><strong>Check the boiler pressure.</strong> Bleeding lets some water out. If the gauge is below 1 bar, top it up. See <a href="/boiler-losing-pressure-liverpool">how to top up boiler pressure</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Turn the heating back on</strong> and check the radiator heats from top to bottom.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If you have several cold radiators, start downstairs and work up. Do the one furthest from the boiler last on each floor.</p>

        <div class="blog-callout">
          <strong>Bleeding the same radiator every few weeks?</strong>
          <p>Air should not keep coming back. If it does, there may be a small leak letting air in, or corrosion inside the system. Both are worth getting checked before they get worse.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Cold at the Bottom: Sludge</h2>
        <p>Over the years, the inside of radiators and pipes slowly rusts. The rust and dirt mix with the water to make a thick black sludge. It is heavy, so it sinks to the bottom of the radiator and blocks the flow.</p>
        <p>Signs of sludge:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Radiators warm at the top but cold along the bottom</li>
          <li>Dark or black water when you bleed a radiator</li>
          <li>Radiators that take a long time to warm up</li>
          <li>A boiler that bangs or makes a kettle-like noise</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Sludge cannot be bled out. The usual fix is a <a href="/power-flushing-liverpool">power flush</a>. We pump cleaning chemicals through the whole system at high flow to wash the sludge out. A magnetic filter on the boiler pipework then catches new rust before it builds up again.</p>
        <p>If only one radiator is badly blocked, we may take that radiator off the wall and flush it outside instead.</p>

        <h2>Cold All Over: A Stuck Valve or Poor Balance</h2>
        <p>If one radiator stays cold while the rest are hot, check the valves first.</p>
        <h3>The thermostatic valve (TRV)</h3>
        <p>This is the valve with numbers on it. Inside is a small pin that can stick down, often after a summer of not being used. Turn the TRV to the highest number. If the radiator still stays cold, the pin may be stuck. A plumber can free it, or fit a new valve.</p>
        <h3>The lockshield valve</h3>
        <p>This is the valve on the other end, usually under a plastic cap. It sets how much water goes through that radiator. If someone has closed it, the radiator will not heat.</p>
        <h3>Radiators far from the boiler are cold</h3>
        <p>Hot water takes the easiest path. If the radiators close to the boiler get too much, the ones furthest away get too little. Setting each lockshield valve so the water is shared out evenly is called balancing. It takes time and a thermometer. We can do it for you.</p>

