        </table>
        <p>All prices include VAT. See our <a href="/radiator-valve-replacement-liverpool">radiator valve</a> and <a href="/power-flushing-liverpool">power flushing</a> pages for the full prices.</p>

        <h2>When to Call a Plumber</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>You have bled the radiator and it is still cold at the top</li>
          <li>The radiator is cold at the bottom, or the water you bled was black</li>
          <li>You need to bleed radiators every few weeks</li>
          <li>The boiler pressure keeps dropping after bleeding</li>
          <li>Water is leaking from a valve or a joint</li>
          <li>The bleed valve will not turn, or will not close</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="blog-warning">
          <strong>Water leaking from a valve that will not stop?</strong>
          <p>Turn the heating off. Close both valves on that radiator if you can. Put a bowl and towels underneath, then call us on <a href="tel:01515580334">0151 558 0334</a>. We answer 24/7.</p>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Want us to take a look?" text="Call for advice or a price. Gas Safe registered, no call-out fee." />

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Why is my radiator cold at the top?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Air is trapped inside it. Air rises, so it sits at the top where hot water cannot reach. Turn the heating off, then bleed the radiator with a radiator key until water comes out. Then check the boiler pressure is still at least 1 bar.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Why is my radiator cold at the bottom?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Usually sludge. Rust and dirt from inside the system settle at the bottom of the radiator and block the flow. Bleeding will not help. The system needs a power flush, or that radiator needs flushing on its own.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Should I bleed radiators with the heating on or off?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Off, and let them cool. Hot water can spit out of the bleed valve and burn you. With the heating off, the air also settles at the top, so more of it comes out.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Why has my boiler pressure dropped after bleeding?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Bleeding lets out air and a little water. On a sealed system, that lowers the pressure. Top it up to 1 to 1.5 bar using the filling loop under the boiler. If it keeps dropping after that, there may be a leak.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">One radiator is cold and the rest are hot. Why?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Check the valve with numbers on it is turned up. If it is, the pin inside may be stuck, or the valve at the other end may be closed. If that radiator is also cold at the bottom, it may be full of sludge.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

